A CONDITIONAL BRANCH CHOOSES WHICH EXCEPTIONAL INSTRUCTION IS NEXT.

Status: bottom-up English Major C example based on an inspected seven-halfword
ARMv7-A T32 diagnostic. The effects of the two encountered exception
instructions have NOT been granted by the current source language profile.
A correct translation stops at an ungranted instruction rather than
inventing a system call, process exit, successful return, or exception
handler. Nothing in this example is a compilation of R, C, or another
high-level function.

ORIGINAL MACHINE BYTES AND THEIR PROVENANCE.

For this diagnostic, the following Thumb assembly source was constructed
by explicitly recording instruction halfwords:

  .syntax unified
  .arch armv7-a
  .thumb
  .text
  .global conditions_fixture
  .thumb_func
  conditions_fixture:
  .hword 0x2000,0xd100,0xd000,0xdefe,0x2103,0xd000,0xdf01

Foreign diagnostic build: clang version 17.0.0, target
armv7a-none-eabi with Thumb state selected; ELF32 little-endian
ARM object. Foreign decode: llvm-objdump version 17.0.0,
--triple=thumbv7-none-eabi. This is evidence of a diagnostic
assembler/disassembler operation and is NOT a maintained
first-party build with ICK or the Android NDK.

The observed external decoder listing, in increasing offset order:

  offset 0x0000, halfword 2000, MOVS R0,#0
  offset 0x0002, halfword D100, BNE with displacement zero
  offset 0x0004, halfword D000, BEQ with displacement zero
  offset 0x0006, halfword DEFE, foreign decoder displays "trap"
  offset 0x0008, halfword 2103, MOVS R1,#3
  offset 0x000A, halfword D000, BEQ with displacement zero
  offset 0x000C, halfword DF01, SVC #1

Each original instruction is TWO BYTES long. A zero-encoded branch
displacement is not the same as a branch to the next two-byte
instruction: its PC operand base is the instruction address plus four.
The mnemonic "trap" at 0x0006 is LLVM's alias for the architecture's
permanently undefined UDF T1 encoding 0xDEFE.

The source evidence is separate from the starting register and flag
values defined in each translation below. The diagnostic decoder did
not run this program under an ARMv7-A CPU with a real OS.

PART ONE. FIRST ENGLISH TRANSLATION STARTING AT OFFSET ZERO.

Given this instruction stream as a hypothetical permitted executable
mapping in T32 instruction state, begin at relative execution index zero.
The initial values of N, Z, C, V and all general registers are unknown.
No asynchronous interrupt or unrelated exception is introduced.
A later instruction must not be said to have executed until a permitted
machine-state transition actually reaches it.

STEP ONE.

At offset 0x0000 the instruction bytes are 0x2000.
This is the T1 MOVS-immediate whose full long English operation name
begins "Write the unsigned eight-bit immediate value explicitly
encoded in this instruction into the selected low general-purpose
register ...". It is currently numbered operation 53.

Write zero to R0. Set N to zero and Z to one. Preserve the previously
unknown C and V. Do not read or write data memory or change LR.
Ordinary execution advances the execution index by TWO BYTES to 0x0002.

After this step:
  R0=0x00000000;
  N=0; Z=1; C and V remain unknown;
  execution index=0x0002.

STEP TWO.

At offset 0x0002 the halfword is 0xD100.
Decode the four-bit sub-opcode as NE, meaning the zero flag is zero.
Decode the imm8 as zero. Its branch displacement is zero bytes.
The architectural PC operand base is offset 0x0006.
If taken, this instruction would choose offset 0x0006.

But Z is one. The NE condition is FALSE.
Do not take the branch. Do not overwrite any general-purpose register
or flags merely by testing the condition. The current instruction is
two bytes, so ordinary sequential execution proceeds to 0x0004.

At this point:
  execution index=0x0004;
  N=0, Z=1, C/V unknown.

STEP THREE.

At offset 0x0004 the halfword is 0xD000.
Its encoded condition is EQ, true when Z is one.
Its immediate is zero, so its encoded displacement is zero.
The architectural PC operand base is the instruction's offset
plus four: 0x0008.

Because Z is one, the branch IS TAKEN, and the new execution
index is 0x0008. The halfword at offset 0x0006
is NOT architecturally executed in this trace.
It remains present in the code bytes and still decodes as UDF
if some other permitted entry path selects it.

Do not claim that the processor "preloaded" the instruction
at 0x0006 as the selected architectural successor.
Any speculative fetch is a distinct hardware matter that
does not constitute a successfully executed UDF instruction.

STEP FOUR.

At offset 0x0008 the halfword 0x2103 is MOVS R1,#3.
It writes the unsigned literal three to R1.
Set N=0 and Z=0. Preserve C and V.
R0 remains zero. R1 becomes 0x00000003.
The next execution index is 0x000A.

STEP FIVE.

At offset 0x000A the halfword 0xD000 again encodes BEQ.
The encoded branch displacement is zero, and its taken
target would be offset 0x000E.

However, Z is zero following MOVS R1,#3.
The branch condition is FALSE.
Ordinary sequential execution goes to 0x000C, not 0x000E.
R0, R1 and all four condition flags are unchanged by the branch.

STEP SIX. STOP AT A KNOWN BUT UNGRANTED INSTRUCTION.

At offset 0x000C the halfword is 0xDF01.
Its high four-bit sub-opcode is hexadecimal F.
This is SVC T1 with encoded immediate byte one.
It is NOT a conditional branch, and it is NOT a
plain English operation authorized by the initial grant.

Stop the checked English Major C trace at 0x000C with:
    status: BLOCKED — native instruction is outside
            the explicit permitted operation set;
    native instruction: SVC T1;
    encoded immediate byte: 1;
    architecture consequence if natively executed:
            supervisor-call exception;
    unverified boundary: OS/ABI exception handling and return.

Do not invent a syscall number, supervisor handler, return address,
process output or arithmetic result. SVC is not simply "invalid user
instruction": an ARM user process can issue it as an architectural
exception request, but this English source profile has not granted
that effect or identified the OS-specific service.

At the blocked boundary the known data state is:
  R0=0;
  R1=3;
  N=0; Z=0;
  C and V remain whatever they were upon entry;
  no data-memory reads or writes have been performed.

The ordered architectural instruction addresses visited in this trace:
    0x0000
    0x0002
    0x0004
    0x0008
    0x000A
    0x000C (decoded but NOT executed by English Major C).

PART TWO. SECOND ENTRY, DIFFERENT FLAGS, SAME MACHINE BYTES.

Choose another declared execution entry at offset 0x0002.
Let Z=0 on entry and leave all other registers and flags unknown.
This is a DIFFERENT model state, not a new version of the bytes.

At offset 0x0002, the same D100 condition NE is now TRUE.
The encoded displacement remains zero.
The architectural PC operand base is offset 0x0006,
so the branch takes the target offset 0x0006.

At offset 0x0006, the halfword DEFE belongs to UDF T1.
The independent diagnostic displays this as "trap",
but the ARM architecture class is permanently undefined.
The initial English Major C operation grant excludes UDF.
Therefore stop, reporting an ungranted undefined-instruction
exception boundary rather than saying the instruction
successfully advanced to 0x0008.

This second path's visited offsets are:
    0x0002
    0x0006 (decoded UDF, English translation blocked).

There is no reason to assert R0=0 in this path:
the MOVS instruction at offset zero was never executed.
There is no reason to assert R1=3:
the instruction at offset 0x0008 was also never executed.

PART THREE. WHAT THE EXAMPLE ESTABLISHES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH.

The example establishes how specific bytes yield specific branch
target calculations and register/flag state changes under assumed
machine inputs. It demonstrates that the same encoded conditional
branch can select different successors without changing its bytes.

It does not establish a real OS exception return, a privilege
transition actually observed on silicon, a working English Major C
parser, or an optimally scheduled implementation. The conditional
branch operations are T1 and do NOT belong inside an IT block.

The two non-branch machine instructions at 0x0006 and 0x000C are
still understood by an ARM decoder. Their exclusion reflects the
language's presently versioned permission contract rather than a
defect in the physical CPU's ability to decode their bit patterns.

The first trace never executes UDF. The second trace never executes
SVC. A true machine-level trace has a causal successor relation;
it is not the same thing as a linear listing of all bytes in memory.

PART FOUR. RETAINED ALTERNATIVES.

Option A: invert the preceding condition and place the ordinary
nontrapping path adjacent to the conditional branch.
Potential advantage: change branch direction and locality.
Cost: requires relocating the actual instructions and recompiling;
the new bytes would not be the original fixture.

Option B: remove or replace the UDF encoding with an ordinary data
operation. This could make a different executable behave differently,
but it is NOT a valid translation of the original machine bytes.
Decision: do not silently substitute.

Option C: model UDF as an explicit typed exception instruction in a
future expanded English Major C ISA grant. Benefit: can translate a
larger portion of real binaries. Cost: must model the exception state
rather than claiming a normal state transition.

Option D: admit SVC with a pinned OS/ABI and explicit capability to
request a supervisor exception. Benefit: can translate actual kernel
service entry from a real executable. Cost: not portable across OSes
without an environment-specific semantics file.

Option E: keep both UDF and SVC as decoded, but ungranted, stopping
points. This is the current profile's choice and permits meaningful
bottom-up progress without inventing missing infrastructure.

No option above justifies declaring a branch-prediction speedup.
No option above should suppress the original assembly bytes or
the reasons a choice was made.

PART FIVE. REJECTION EXAMPLES FOR INDEPENDENT ACCEPTANCE.

A verifier of these intended source semantics must reject any trace
which:
  * takes the BNE at offset 0x0002 after MOVS zero set Z=1;
  * refuses to take the BEQ at offset 0x0004 with Z=1;
  * treats the branch's zero displacement as an A+2 target;
  * executes the skipped DEFE on the first trace;
  * treats LLVM's "trap" spelling as a new permitted operation;
  * leaves Z=1 after executing MOVS R1,#3;
  * takes the BEQ at offset 0x000A after Z becomes zero;
  * pretends DF01 is a signed-byte displacement;
  * claims that an OS service was performed at DF01;
  * substitutes a successful NOP for either SVC or UDF;
  * labels absent register values as zeros;
  * treats a branch predicted by hardware as proof it executed;
  * claims this diagnostic was executed on an ARM process.

END OF THE SOURCE-DERIVED TRACE.
